%I A400968 #13 Oct 18 2026 17:13:04
%S A400968 5,11,13,3,-27,-85,-179,-317,-507,-757,-1075,-1469,-1947,-2517,-3187,
%T A400968 -3965,-4859,-5877,-7027,-8317,-9755,-11349,-13107,-15037,-17147,
%U A400968 -19445,-21939,-24637,-27547,-30677,-34035,-37629,-41467,-45557,-49907,-54525,-59419,-64597,-70067
%N A400968 a(n) is the number of boundary lattice points minus the number of interior lattice points of the octahedron |x| + |y| + |z| <= n.
%C A400968 The octahedron has A001845(n) lattice points, of which A001845(n-1) are interior and A005899(n) are on the boundary. a(n) is the boundary count minus the interior count.
%C A400968 a(n) reaches a maximum of 13 at n = 3 and is negative for n >= 5, since the interior count grows like n^3 while the boundary count grows like n^2.
%H A400968 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400968/b400968.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400968 <a href="/index/Rec#order_04">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (4,-6,4,-1).
%F A400968 a(n) = (-4*n^3 + 18*n^2 - 8*n + 9)/3.
%F A400968 a(n) = A005899(n) - A001845(n-1).
%F A400968 a(n) = A001845(n) - 2*A001845(n-1).
%F A400968 G.f.: x*(5 - 9*x - x^2 - 3*x^3)/(1 - x)^4.
%F A400968 E.g.f.: exp(x)*(3 + 2*x + 2*x^2 - 4*x^3/3) - 3. - _Stefano Spezia_, Oct 11 2026
%e A400968 For n = 3 the octahedron has 63 lattice points, of which 25 are interior and 38 are on the boundary, so a(3) = 38 - 25 = 13.
%t A400968 A400968[n_] := 3 - 2*(n - 4)*n*(2*n - 1)/3; Array[A400968, 50] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 18 2026 *)
%o A400968 (PARI) a(n) = (-4*n^3 + 18*n^2 - 8*n + 9)/3
%o A400968 (Python) def a(n): return (-4*n**3 + 18*n**2 - 8*n + 9)//3
%Y A400968 Compare A400232 (cube) and A400967 (regular tetrahedron).
%Y A400968 Cf. A001845, A005899, A342831, A400232, A400967.
%K A400968 sign,easy,new
%O A400968 1,1
%A A400968 _Henrik Arhold_, Oct 11 2026
